-- title:
--   Each coordinate is bounded by the maximum norm
-- statement:
--   For every $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and every coordinate index $i \in \{1,\dots,d\}$,
--   $$|x_i| \;\le\; \|x\|_\infty,
--   \qquad\text{where}\quad \|x\|_\infty = \sup_j |x_j| .$$
--
--   This is the elimination rule for the maximum norm, dual to the introduction rule that derives a bound on $\|x\|_\infty$ from coordinatewise bounds: it is how membership of a cube is turned back into information about individual coordinates.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The maximum norm is defined as the indexed supremum $\bigsqcup_j |x_j|$ over the finite index type `Fin d`; the supremum of a finite non-empty family of reals is attained, which is what makes the bound hold with no side condition.
